$ whycant -u apache read /srv/www/index.html

✓ apache (uid 48) can read /srv/www/index.html

  /                     drwxr-xr-x  root:root  ✓ traverse
  /srv                  drwxr-xr-x  root:root  ✓ traverse
  /srv/www              drwxr-xr-x  root:root  ✓ traverse
  /srv/www/index.html   -rw-r--r--  root:root  ! read

Also noticed
  /srv/www/index.html: SELinux label differs from the policy default
    /srv/www/index.html is labeled unconfined_u:object_r:user_home_t:s0, but the policy expects system_u:object_r:httpd_sys_content_t:s0.
    Files moved (mv) from elsewhere keep their old label, which confined services are
    often not allowed to use.
  /srv/www/index.html: SELinux is permissive but logged denials
    SELinux is permissive, so it only logs. These denials for /srv/www/index.html would block access
    in enforcing mode:
      type=AVC msg=audit(1761650000.123:412): avc:  denied  { read } for  pid=1234 comm="httpd" name="index.html" dev="dm-0" ino=4242 scontext=system_u:system_r:httpd_t:s0 tcontext=unconfined_u:object_r:user_home_t:s0 tclass=file permissive=0
      type=AVC msg=audit(1761650100.555:420): avc:  denied  { getattr } for  pid=1234 comm="httpd" path="/srv/www/index.html" dev="dm-0" ino=4242 scontext=system_u:system_r:httpd_t:s0 tcontext=unconfined_u:object_r:user_home_t:s0 tclass=file permissive=0
